  "query_preview": "..The east wind event is well underway with max\nwind gusts since midnight ranging from 50 mph to a few isolated\ngusts of 80 mph. The strongest gusts 70-80 mph have all been on\nthe northwest side of mountains, mainly in the Saddlebrooke\narea, Kielberg Canyon in the Galiuros, and in the Santa Ritas.\nThese winds have also led to areas of blowing dust and haze\nacross southeast Arizona.\n\nAt the surface a very strong area of high pressure is centered \nover eastern Colorado and will slowly move southward. The \npressure gradient will remain very strong over the region \nthrough Saturday afternoon then begin to weaken. There is also a\ncutoff low centered just off the coast of northern Baja that \nwill remain over that general area into Sunday.\n\nThis afternoon winds have weakened some thanks to daytime mixing\nand perhaps less mountain wave action as the ridgetop inversion\nweakens. However this will be shortlived as winds will increase\nagain after the sun goes down with sustained speeds generally\n30-45 mph and gusts 50-75 mph. Again the strongest winds are\nexpected to be on the northwest side of mountain ranges as the\ninversion reasserts itself allowing for mountain wave trapping\nand downsloping. REFS probabilities for 65+ mph wind gusts for \nthese areas this evening and tonight are 30-70 percent. No \nchanges to the Wind Advisory or High Wind Warning. The HWW is in\neffect until 5 AM Saturday and Wind Advisory is in effect from \n5 AM Saturday to 2 PM Saturday. \n\nThe cutoff low near Baja has started to draw up moisture from\nthe remnants of Simon as evidenced by showers and thunderstorms\ncurrently ongoing in northern Sonora. Hi-res models keep this\nactivity south of the international border today, but can't rule\nout a weak shower or two over western Pima County this afternoon\nor evening. Saturday moisture continues to stream in and rain\nchances increase as the cutoff low edges eastward. Rain and\nthunderstorms chances will mainly be confined to areas from\nTucson west Saturday. Sunday rain chances expand to cover most \nof southeast Arizona. QPF looks relatively light with totals \nSaturday-Sunday up to 0.25\" possible. \n\nNext week the moisture sticks around and the closed low over \nBaja opens up and flattens out. At the same time a Pacific low\nmoves into the Pacific Northwest then moves south along the west\ncoast. This system will tap into the moisture leading to\ncontinued daily rain and thunderstorm chances for most of next\nweek. Thanks to the progressive pattern high temperatures stay \nbelow normal over the next 7 days.",
